From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 13:38:05 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] llvmpipe: keep linear mode on a CPU without SSE2

llvmpipe's linear mode, for one 8-bit color buffer with no depth
buffer and one viewport, does three things: draw clips x and y only to
a guard band, setup scissors to the viewport, and setup draws two
triangles that form a screen-aligned rectangle as one rectangle with
one set of interpolants. The mode also lets the linear rasterizer take
such rectangles, and that rasterizer needs SSE2, so llvmpipe left the
whole mode off on every other CPU, arm64 included.

Without it the two triangles of a quad get separate planes that differ
in the last bit, so an attribute that lands exactly on a texel boundary
resolves differently on either side of the diagonal. A quad that leaves
the viewport is cut into a polygon whose triangles differ the same way.
dEQP-GLES3.functional.fbo.blit.rect.nearest_consistency_* blit with
GL_NEAREST at scales such as 6/7, where every seventh row of samples
sits on a source texel boundary, and all 24 cases failed on arm64.

Keep the mode wherever its framebuffer conditions hold, and ask for
SSE2 only where the scene picks the linear rasterizer. Nothing changes
on x86.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/gallium/drivers/llvmpipe/lp_setup.c         | 7 ++++---
 src/gallium/drivers/llvmpipe/lp_state_derived.c | 9 +++++----
 2 files changed, 9 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/llvmpipe/lp_setup.c b/src/gallium/drivers/llvmpipe/lp_setup.c
index 527d372ce5c9fcdd60c279d45c6d203fe1ee920d..a4b6e9425b6eb62523660e0aa1ff73032b328ed4 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/llvmpipe/lp_setup.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/llvmpipe/lp_setup.c
@@ -112,7 +112,9 @@ lp_setup_get_empty_scene(struct lp_setup_context *setup)
    }
 
    setup->scene = setup->scenes[i];
-   setup->scene->permit_linear_rasterizer = setup->permit_linear_rasterizer;
+   setup->scene->permit_linear_rasterizer =
+      setup->permit_linear_rasterizer &&
+      lp_setup_linear_rasterizer_supported();
 
    /* XXX: doing that here is ugly... */
    setup->scene->fb_max_samples = util_framebuffer_get_num_samples(&setup->fb);
@@ -876,8 +878,7 @@ void
 lp_setup_set_linear_mode(struct lp_setup_context *setup,
                          bool mode)
 {
-   setup->permit_linear_rasterizer = mode &&
-                                     lp_setup_linear_rasterizer_supported();
+   setup->permit_linear_rasterizer = mode;
 }
 
 
diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/llvmpipe/lp_state_derived.c b/src/gallium/drivers/llvmpipe/lp_state_derived.c
index 3a2dcace4089091fca37d8819f4010445730a37a..90ac8a287a73c5a44d905b4a84e3dcb4e36e8c14 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/llvmpipe/lp_state_derived.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/llvmpipe/lp_state_derived.c
@@ -236,13 +236,14 @@ check_linear_rasterizer(struct llvmpipe_context *lp)
         lp->framebuffer.cbufs[0].format == PIPE_FORMAT_R8G8B8X8_UNORM));
 
    /* permit_linear means guardband, hence fake scissor, which we can only
-    * handle if there's just one vp, and only when the linear rasterizer is
-    * available, since setup scissors to the viewport only in that case. */
+    * handle if there's just one vp, since setup scissors to the viewport
+    * only in that case. It also lets setup draw a screen-aligned rectangle
+    * as one, with one set of interpolants. Neither needs SSE2; only the
+    * linear rasterizer itself does, which the scene checks. */
    const bool single_vp = lp->viewport_index_slot < 0;
    const bool permit_linear = (!lp->framebuffer.zsbuf.texture &&
                                valid_cb_format &&
-                               single_vp &&
-                               lp_setup_linear_rasterizer_supported());
+                               single_vp);
 
    /* Tell draw that we're happy doing our own x/y clipping.
     */
